<<~ ahu slot=`#/invitation`>>
! Example Two ~ the photograph that needs no Folio

A sparse Inscription can express a complete photographic invitation without selecting a camera, paper, crop, lighting recipe or supporting research board. Only subject and read need be specific; the other dimensions remain delegated to the canonical `art()` defaults.
<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/inscription`>>
```tiddlywiki
<<art worktype=`single observational photograph` subject=`an empty public bench after rainfall` read=`human presence suggested through absence, without inventing its occupants`>>
```
<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/critical-reading`>>
A successful interpretation may use the bench, wet surfaces, and surrounding quiet to suggest absence. It need not depict the photographer, a departed person, or the source photograph as an object inside another frame. Avoid inventing a specific place, person, or documentary provenance. Different lighting or lenses could fulfill the same invitation. If photographic evidence is claimed, the receiver's synthetic or actual-capture status must be named outside the sigil.
<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/noninheritance`>>
Do not infer that benches, rain, documentary realism, human absence or a single central subject are defaults for `art()`. This case illustrates the value of omission, not a universal preference for sparsity.
<<~/ahu>>

